  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "If \\((a,b)\\) and \\((c,d)\\) are two points on the line whose equation is \\(y=mx+k,\\) then the distance between \\((a,b)\\) and \\((c,d),\\) in terms of \\(a,\\) \\(c,\\) and \\(m,\\) is",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(|a-c|\\sqrt{1+m^2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(|a+c|\\sqrt{1+m^2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{|a-c|}{\\sqrt{1+m^2}}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(|a-c|(1+m^2)\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(|a-c||m|\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "a",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1974-p11.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Since both points are on the line, \\(b-d=m(a-c).\\) Their distance is therefore \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\ell&=\\sqrt{(a-c)^2+(b-d)^2}\\\\ &=\\sqrt{(a-c)^2(1+m^2)}\\\\ &=|a-c|\\sqrt{1+m^2}. \\end{aligned} \\]\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A.",
      "hints": [
        "Use the line equation to express \\(b-d\\) in terms of \\(a-c\\)",
        "Substitute \\(b-d=m(a-c)\\) into the distance formula"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
